      "description": "### Summary\r\n\r\n`Postgrex.Notifications` in the `postgrex` escapes double quotes in channel names but not PostgreSQL's dollar-quote delimiter (`$$`). An attacker who can influence a single `LISTEN` channel name (for example via untrusted tenant or user identifiers) can inject `$$` into the reconnect replay query that the notification process builds on every (re)connect. The corrupted query is rejected by PostgreSQL on each connect, silently dropping notifications for every channel sharing that connection, a denial of service against all tenants on that connection. The existing escaping of double-quote happens to prevent forming a fully valid injected statement, so arbitrary SQL execution is not achievable.\r\n\r\n### Details\r\n\r\n1. Set up an application that uses `Postgrex.Notifications` and passes untrusted input (a tenant or user identifier) as the channel name to `Postgrex.Notifications.listen/3`.\r\n2. Register a benign channel on the shared connection (e.g. `tenant_42`).\r\n3. As the attacker, call `Postgrex.Notifications.listen/3` with a crafted channel name containing the dollar-quote delimiter, e.g. `x$$;SELECT 1;--` (15 bytes, no null byte, so it passes the `listen/3` guards).\r\n4. On the next (auto-)reconnect, `handle_connect/1` replays both subscriptions inside a single `DO $$BEGIN ... END$$` block. The attacker's `$$` closes the block early, PostgreSQL parses the remainder as extra statements and rejects the whole query with a syntax error.\r\n5. The replay fails on every reconnect, so notifications stop being delivered for all channels on that connection.\r\n\r\n### Impact\r\n\r\nAn attacker who can influence a single channel name passed to `Postgrex.Notifications.listen/3` can corrupt the reconnect replay query for the entire connection, causing it to fail on every connect and silently drop PostgreSQL notifications for all channels (all tenants) sharing that connection. Any application that derives channel names from untrusted input is affected, no privileges beyond the ability to influence one channel name are required. Such applications must either upgrade or ensure the user input does not contain the `$` character.\r\n\r\n### References\r\n\r\n* Introduction commit: https://github.com/elixir-ecto/postgrex/commit/266b530faf9bde094e31e0e4ab851f933fadc0f5\r\n* Patch commit: https://github.com/elixir-ecto/postgrex/commit/797d395906c0a1d28bea55e3a2b03ce75c827f55\r\n",
